00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:33.681
reusable rocket.

40
00:02:34.381 --> 00:02:38.389
reusable rocket means a rocket that can land and fly again.

41
00:02:38.689 --> 00:02:41.989
Reusable rockets have made launches much cheaper.

42
00:02:42.689 --> 00:02:46.084
Your turn. Say: reusable rocket.

43
00:02:48.784 --> 00:02:50.075
reusable rocket.

44
00:02:51.083 --> 00:02:53.327
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:53.627 --> 00:02:56.903
Rithy, is space exploration worth the cost?

46
00:02:58.217 --> 00:03:01.760
Given the problems on Earth, I'm not always sure.

47
00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:08.849
A fair point. Once considered science fiction, commercial spaceflight is now routine.

48
00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:12.742
Thanks to reusable rockets, I suppose?

49
00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:20.499
Exactly. Seen from a scientific perspective, the discovery of exoplanets is extraordinary.

50
00:03:21.783 --> 00:03:25.675
And astrobiology might one day prove we're not alone.

51
00:03:26.983 --> 00:03:33.142
But space debris is becoming a serious problem. If unchecked, it could make some orbits unusable.

52
00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:39.340
So exploration should be balanced with responsibility, just like on Earth.

53
00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:45.343
Grammar focus: Participle and verbless clauses (extended).

54
00:03:45.643 --> 00:03:49.512
Participle and verbless clauses make formal arguments concise.

55
00:03:49.812 --> 00:03:56.017
Given the problems on Earth, I'm unsure. Once considered fiction, spaceflight is now routine.

56
00:03:56.317 --> 00:03:58.885
If unchecked, space debris could spread.

57
00:03:59.185 --> 00:04:03.588
These structures add sophistication and conciseness to formal arguments.

58
00:04:04.583 --> 00:04:07.630
Now read and listen. Why Go to Space?.

59
00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:16.664
Launched in 1957, the first artificial satellite began a new era of exploration.

60
00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:22.457
The space race between superpowers soon led to astronauts walking on the Moon.

61
00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:28.826
Today, commercial spaceflight companies compete to reduce costs with reusable rockets.

62
00:04:30.117 --> 00:04:35.379
Several nations are planning a lunar base as a step towards Mars colonisation.

63
00:04:36.683 --> 00:04:42.456
Meanwhile, telescopes have detected thousands of exoplanets, some possibly habitable.

64
00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:47.526
Critics argue that the money would be better spent solving problems on Earth.

65
00:04:48.817 --> 00:04:56.134
Supporters reply that space research has produced technologies we use every day, from GPS to weather forecasting.

66
00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:02.375
If managed responsibly, space exploration could benefit all of humanity.

67
00:05:03.683 --> 00:05:06.289
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:06.589 --> 00:05:10.528
A planet orbiting a star other than our Sun is an blank.

69
00:05:10.828 --> 00:05:15.770
A, exoplanet. B, lunar base. C, space debris.

70
00:05:19.783 --> 00:05:22.421
The answer is A: exoplanet.

71
00:05:23.917 --> 00:05:26.381
blank the cost, the project was cancelled.

72
00:05:26.681 --> 00:05:30.706
A, Given. B, Gave. C, Giving to.

73
00:05:34.717 --> 00:05:37.111
The answer is A: Given.

74
00:05:38.617 --> 00:05:44.192
blank in 1957, the satellite changed history.

75
00:05:44.492 --> 00:05:48.262
A, Launching. B, Launched. C, Launch.

76
00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:54.540
The answer is B: Launched.

77
00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:58.955
The study of possible life beyond Earth is blank.

78
00:05:59.255 --> 00:06:05.486
A, astrobiology. B, space race. C, Mars colonisation.

79
00:06:09.483 --> 00:06:12.307
The answer is A: astrobiology.

80
00:06:13.817 --> 00:06:20.112
According to the text, which everyday technologies came from space research?

81
00:06:20.412 --> 00:06:27.827
A, GPS and weather forecasting. B, smartphones only. C, electric cars.

82
00:06:31.817 --> 00:06:35.929
The answer is A: GPS and weather forecasting.

83
00:06:37.417 --> 00:06:42.728
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should governments spend money on space exploration?

84
00:06:48.028 --> 00:06:55.368
Part two: Use a participle clause: give your view, two reasons and one counter-argument.

85
00:07:01.668 --> 00:07:13.727
Considering its benefits, I think they should, within limits. Space research improves communication and climate monitoring. Given the urgent problems on Earth, however, budgets should be balanced carefully.

86
00:07:14.027 --> 00:07:16.398
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:17.383 --> 00:07:23.716
Great work today! You learned eight new words and participle and verbless clauses (extended).

88
00:07:24.016 --> 00:07:28.038
Today's value is wonder. Look up and wonder at the world.

89
00:07:28.338 --> 00:07:31.873
See you in Unit twenty-six. Goodbye!

90
00:07:32.173 --> 00:07:34.860
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

